<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-gcd" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Greatest Common Divisor</title>

<p>If a number <m>y</m> is divisible by a number <m>x</m>, then we call <m>x</m> a divisor of <m>y</m>. 
</p>

<p>If <m>x</m> is a divisor of <m>y</m> and <m>z</m>, then we say it is a common divisor of <m>y</m> and <m>z</m>. 
</p>

<example>
    <title>Common Divisors of 30 and 45</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        What are the positive common divisors of 30 and 45?
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        We first make a list of the positive divisors of 30:
      </p>
      <md>1, 2, 3, 5, 6, 10, 15, 30.</md>

      <p>
        Then we make a list of the positive divisors of 45:
      </p>
      <md>1, 3, 5, 9, 15, 45.</md>
      
      <p> The common divisors of 30 and 45 are the numbers that appear in both lists; 1, 3, 5, and 15.</p>
    </solution>
  </example>

  <p>The greatest common divisor of numbers <m>y</m> and <m>z</m> is the largest among their common divisors. This is 
  often represented as <m>\gcd(y,z)</m>. </p>

  <example>
    <title>Greatest Common Divisor of 30 and 45</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Calculate <m>\gcd(30,45)</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p> We previously determined that the common divisors are: 1, 3, 5, and 15. The greatest among these is 15,
        so <m>\gcd(30,45)=15</m>.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </example>

  <exercise label="ww-gcd">
    <title>Greatest Common Divisor</title>
    <webwork>
        <pg-code>
            # Set the math environment to expect fraction format answers
            Context("Fraction-NoDecimals");
            
            # Define the exact answer
            $gcd = 6;
        </pg-code>
        <statement>
            <p>
                <m>\gcd(42,24)=</m><var name="$gcd" width="10"/>
            </p>
        </statement>
    </webwork>
</exercise>

